  "lean": {
    "statement": "∀ {E : Type u_1}\n  [inst : NormedAddCommGroup E] [inst_1 : InnerProductSpace ℝ E] [inst_2 : FiniteDimensional ℝ E]\n  [inst_3 : MeasurableSpace E] [inst_4 : BorelSpace E] (W : E → ℝ),\n  ContDiff ℝ 1 W →\n    MeasureTheory.Integrable (fun x => Real.exp (-W x)) MeasureTheory.MeasureSpace.volume →\n      have μ := MeasureTheory.MeasureSpace.volume.tilted fun x => -W x;\n      ∃ D,\n        Dense ↑D.domain ∧\n          D.IsClosable ∧\n            D.closure.IsClosed ∧\n              ∀ (u : ↥(MeasureTheory.Lp ℝ 2 μ)) (v : ↥(MeasureTheory.Lp E 2 μ)),\n                (u, v) ∈ D.graph ↔ ∃ f, ContDiff ℝ (↑⊤) f ∧ HasCompactSupport f ∧ ↑↑u =ᵐ[μ] f ∧ ↑↑v =ᵐ[μ] gradient f",
    "statement_sha256": "60a4223280f49c9d9c403eda7b21d8faea3c0e2258e26c2409df4d003050338b",
    "compiled": true,
    "approved_definition_context": [
      "E is a finite-dimensional real inner-product space with Borel measurable structure and canonical volume, including dimension zero.",
      "ContDiff at order1/2/infinity means C1/C2/smooth. HasCompactSupport means closure of nonzero support is compact. gradient is the Riesz representative of the genuine Frechet derivative.",
      "Lp F 2 mu is the vector space of measurable functions with finite squared-norm integral, modulo mu-almost-everywhere equality. Equality =ae explicitly compares representatives. Dense uses the L2 norm topology.",
      "A LinearPMap is a partial real-linear map with a linear-subspace domain. Its graph consists of input-output pairs on that domain. IsClosable means the topological closure of that graph is itself a graph of a partial linear map. For a closable map its closure has exactly that closed graph; IsClosed means graph-closed, not boundedness or an everywhere-defined map.",
      "Measure.tilted is normalized exponential weighting, totalized to zero when the exponential is not integrable. The displayed Integrable condition means almost-everywhere strong measurability and finite integral of norm.",
      "Measure.map is pushforward; Measure.prod encodes independent product; stdGaussian is the standard Gaussian. A Markov kernel is a measurably parameterized probability measure. IsCondKernel disintegrates the specified joint measure on its first coordinate; Prod.swap swaps coordinates.",
      "Existential operators are chosen before the universal quantifiers over their graph inputs. Lets abbreviate the displayed actual objects, not additional assumptions."
    ]
  },
